{
  "_doc": "Project profile — written by /setup:start after the bootstrap interview, committed. Machine-readable mirror of the answers; `CLAUDE.md → ### Branch model` and `Language Rules` are the human-readable mirror and are filled from the same values. Consumers: /setup:create-CLAUDE_MD (workflow, language), /pr-create (git_host), /maintain:sync-from-starter (excluded paths + which .mcp.json / settings.json entries to skip). Copy to project-profile.json only via /setup:start — never by hand.",

  "schema": 1,

  "_mode_doc": "greenfield | brownfield. Detected from the project file count (>= 50 files => brownfield), confirmed in the interview.",
  "mode": "greenfield",

  "_git_host_doc": "github | bitbucket | gitlab | none. Detected from `origin`, confirmed. `none` = no remote yet; PR credentials are deferred until one exists.",
  "git_host": "github",

  "_workflow_doc": "Asked: preset + pr_required (Screen 1, Q3) and team_merge (Screen 2, Q7). Everything else is DERIVED once from the preset table in create-CLAUDE_MD → Identify Git Workflow and never asked. preset is one of trunk | feature-branch | gitflow — `custom` is not a profile value; a custom workflow goes through /setup:create-CLAUDE_MD instead. pr_required=false (\"I push straight to main\") overrides pr_dest / protected / merge as shown here.",
  "workflow": {
    "_preset_doc": "trunk | feature-branch | gitflow",
    "preset": "trunk",
    "pr_required": false,
    "trunk": "main",
    "integration": "main",
    "branch_types": ["feat", "fix", "chore", "refactor"],
    "_branch_pattern_doc": "Derived from the tracker answer: jira => <type>/<KEY>-<slug>, none => <type>/<slug>.",
    "branch_pattern": "<type>/<slug>",
    "pr_dest": "commit on main or short-lived branches merged locally — no PR",
    "protected": [],
    "_merge_doc": "squash | merge-commit | ff. `ff` only for the direct-push override; otherwise the preset's implied value.",
    "merge": "ff",
    "_team_merge_doc": "confirm | auto. Asked by /setup:start (Screen 2, Q7); absent = confirm. confirm: team-setup.sh (/setup:teams) puts team-merge-check.sh, team-integrate.sh, team-integrate-lock.sh and team-starter-land.sh behind ask, so each of those merge steps waits for a click. auto: team-setup.sh leaves them out and removes them when present — for every session that uses the project settings, the team roles in the clones included (the scripts' own refusals still apply). It gates only these scripts: the Azure DevOps merge (/pr-merge) and the Supervisor's auto-merge decision (supervisor-sessions.md → Auto-merge) do not read it. Role sessions run with a materialized copy of the ask list: restart a running one after a change.",
    "team_merge": "confirm",
    "_jira_transition_doc": "Opt-in Jira transition guard (.claude/hooks/guard-jira-transition.sh). jira_transition_comment: true → a jira_transition_issue call without a comment is refused. jira_transition_evidence: {\"<PROJECT>:<transition id>\": \"<case-insensitive ERE>\"} — that transition's comment must match, e.g. {\"PROJ:31\": \"Release-[0-9]+|no deploy\"}; ids are workflow-scoped, hence the project prefix. \"*:<transition id>\" applies to every project without its own string key (the exact key wins; an exact \"\" opts a project out). Default: off, no rules.",
    "jira_transition_comment": false,
    "jira_transition_evidence": {},
    "_tracker_comments_doc": "Opt-in: true → ticket comments are requirements (.agents/reference/tracker-comments.md) — /brainstorm and /quick-change snapshot them, /execute, /check-implementation and /pr-merge check for new ones. Default: off, no extra tracker reads.",
    "tracker_comments": false,
    "_pr_open_doc": "Opt-in: true → /pr-create opens the PR (github: gh, azure-devops: az) after showing the full draft and an explicit Create answer, with your local CLI identity. Default: off — the command only prepares the PR for you to open.",
    "pr_open": false,
    "_guarded_writes_doc": "Opt-in: true → .claude/lib/guarded-writes/{az-write,ops-write,sqlcmd-write}.sh run allow-listed writes under the write profile ~/.azure-write, from .claude/guarded-writes.json once that file is on origin/<Trunk>. The Operator also sets read-only default identities and pastes the settings block (.agents/reference/guarded-writes.md). Default: off — the wrappers refuse.",
    "guarded_writes": false,
    "_local_env_doc": "Opt-in: true → .claude/lib/local-env/with-local-env.sh runs a runner registered in .claude/local-env.json (once that file is on origin/<Trunk>) with one local dev secret from ~/.config/<dir>/local.env, given only to that child's env and masked in its output. The Operator writes the file with local-env-init.sh and pastes the settings block (.agents/reference/local-env-runner.md). Default: off — the runner refuses.",
    "local_env": false,
    "_remote_build_doc": "Opt-in: true → teams.build_check can be \"bash \\\"$HARNESS_MAIN/.claude/lib/remote-build/remote-build-check.sh\\\"\": the build/test command runs on a remote node over ssh (never locally), from .claude/remote-build.json once that file is on origin/<Trunk> — the node is chosen only through trunk. The node must be a machine for untrusted code (.agents/reference/remote-build.md). Default: off — the check refuses.",
    "remote_build": false,
    "_finder_fallback_doc": "session | off. /check-implementation Step 1ab: when a pinned finder (@correctness-reviewer, @structure-reviewer) fails twice on a rate or usage limit or an unavailable model, session re-runs it once as its -fallback agent on the session's model (model: inherit, same effort, same read-only contract); the report and the team QA Warnings name it. off → STOP instead. Absent = session. Any other finder failure, or a model substitution seen by finder-model.sh, always stops.",
    "finder_fallback": "session",
    "_subagent_model_doc": "Opt-in: enabled true → the subagent-model mod (.claude/skills/subagent-model/) lowers a built-in subagent spawned without a model to the table's alias (haiku | sonnet | opus) when the session's model is dearer; never raises, never touches pinned, non-built-in, forked, resumed, teammate or workflow agents. No table → the default below; a table replaces it whole. One runtime-log line per changed spawn (.agents/reference/token-usage.md → Runtime log). Default: off.",
    "subagent_model": {
      "enabled": false,
      "table": { "general-purpose": "opus", "claude": "opus", "Explore": "opus" }
    }
  },

  "_tracker_doc": "jira | github | none. Asked (Screen 2, Q4). none => the backlog is .agents/backlog.md. github => the tasks are the issues of github.repo; .claude/lib/team-tracker.sh reads them without a teams block.",
  "tracker": "none",

  "_github_doc": "Only for tracker=github (and read by the team layer when teams.github is absent: one block for both paths; teams.github wins when both exist). repo: owner/repo, required. The Project fields — project_owner, project_number, project_id, status_field_id, status_options.started/.delivered — are all present or all omitted: omitted = No Project, and status moves are skipped. IDs once with `gh project field-list <number> --owner <owner> --format json`. Teams always need the Project. Neutral example values.",
  "github": {
    "repo": "owner/repo",
    "project_owner": "owner",
    "project_number": 1,
    "project_id": "PVT_example",
    "status_field_id": "PVTSSF_example",
    "status_options": {"started": "opt-started-example", "delivered": "opt-delivered-example"}
  },

  "_qa_doc": "Optional. pool_status: the Jira status name or GitHub Project Status option that means waiting for QA — /qa-verify with no argument reads the tickets in it. Asked by /setup:start after Q4 (skippable); absent => the first pool run of /qa-verify asks once and writes it with a decisions.md entry.",
  "qa": {"pool_status": "Ready for QA"},

  "_status_report_doc": "OPTIONAL, NOT asked by /setup:start; add by hand. Read by /tasks-status only. jira_jql: the JQL that picks the tasks the report covers when the tracker is jira; omitted => `assignee = currentUser() AND statusCategory != Done ORDER BY status, key` (my open issues, across projects) and the report says it used that default.",
  "status_report": {
    "jira_jql": "project = PROJ AND statusCategory != Done ORDER BY status, key"
  },

  "_confluence_doc": "Asked (Screen 2, Q5). Shares the Atlassian MCP server and token with Jira.",
  "confluence": false,

  "_codex_doc": "Asked (Screen 2, Q6) as a TEAM question. `command -v codex` on the bootstrap machine is informational only and never sets this field — the file is shared.",
  "codex": false,

  "_app_surface_doc": "unknown | none | web | mobile | desktop | tui. NOT asked by /setup:start — always written as `unknown`, which keeps all QA tooling. /setup:create-CLAUDE_MD detects it from the stack once code exists.",
  "app_surface": "unknown",

  "_language_doc": "pl | en. Asked first (Screen 0). Governs Claude <-> developer communication only; code, comments and commits stay English.",
  "language": "pl",

  "_teams_doc": "Values the team harness (/start-team, .claude/lib/team-*.sh) reads instead of hardcoding them. Written by /setup:teams; a project without this block refuses /start-team before any side effect. Read with `jq` only — never eval'd or sourced. tracker: jira | github | none (sets the ticket-key regex; none = local `L<n>` keys allocated by team-tracker.sh, zero tracker calls). github: REQUIRED when tracker=github or pr_provider=github, ignored otherwise; absent => the top-level github block is used (the whole block, never a per-field merge) — repo (`owner/repo`), project_owner, project_number plus the Project v2 IDs project_id, status_field_id and status_options.started/.delivered (read the IDs once with `gh project field-list <number> --owner <owner> --format json`). pr_provider: azure-devops | github | none (none = the PR layer is off: setup, the channel, the operator kit and the supervision roles work, team runs, PRs and merges refuse naming the setting); github.repo is required for pr_provider=github too. trunk: the line team branches are cut from (base-ref = origin/<trunk>). branch_types: allowed <type>/<key>-<slug> prefixes. required_tools: must be on PATH before a team starts; the session host (tmux or Herdr) is checked separately. clone_parent: OPTIONAL — where team clones live; omit it for the default (siblings of this repo). A leading ~ is expanded and spaces are allowed. clone_name: OPTIONAL — the clone directory template; omit it for the default `<repo dir>-{team}`. `{team}` is the team name, `{Team}` its capitalized form, and the placeholder must END the template (exactly one, no `/`) because the clone glob is a prefix. seed_files: copied into every clone (.env is chmod 600 when listed). env_file_required: refuse to start without a mode-600 .env. build_check: the build command, run with `bash -c` in the clone root. test_gate: a script path, or `none` to skip the gate. gates: auto | manual-mql5 (manual-mql5 = the gates are run by hand and the QA report says so). Values below are neutral examples — not any project's real ones.",
  "teams": {
    "tracker": "github",
    "github": {
      "repo": "owner/repo",
      "project_owner": "owner",
      "project_number": 1,
      "project_id": "PVT_example",
      "status_field_id": "PVTSSF_example",
      "status_options": {"started": "opt-started", "delivered": "opt-delivered"}
    },
    "pr_provider": "github",
    "trunk": "main",
    "branch_types": ["feature", "bugfix", "chore"],
    "clone_name": "<repo>-{team}",
    "required_tools": ["git", "jq", "claude", "codex", "gh"],
    "seed_files": [".claude/settings.local.json", ".agents/memory/user-profile.md"],
    "starter_root": null,
    "_starter_root_doc": "OPTIONAL — only in the project that develops the starter itself: the path (relative to the repo root) of a local checkout of the starter, e.g. `../<starter checkout>`. Set → team-starter-land.sh lands a team's starter worktree branch on the starter's `main` before the team's merge; absent/null → the helper refuses (exit 2). Every other project leaves it out.",
    "env_file_required": false,
    "build_check": "echo 'no build check configured'",
    "test_gate": "none",
    "gates": "auto",
    "_session_prefix_doc": "OPTIONAL — the prefix of every session name the session host starts: <prefix>-<team>-<role>, <prefix>-<role>, <prefix>-<helper>, so two checkouts on one machine never collide. Lowercase, [a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-], at most 12 characters; another value stops the start with the key named. Empty = not set: channel.tmux_prefix, else the repo dir slug, lowercased and cut to 12 with a warning. A team name whose longest session name exceeds 32 characters is refused by team-new.sh.",
    "session_prefix": "",
    "_session_host_doc": "OPTIONAL — where sessions run: auto | tmux | herdr (default auto). herdr: herdr-core through `hc` (its core must answer `hc server status`, else a start stops with exit 6). auto: Herdr when its core answers, else tmux (exit 6 with neither). A running session stays on the backend it was started on. TEAM_SESSION_HOST overrides this key. A session host (tmux or Herdr) is checked at team start, so tmux need not be in required_tools.",
    "session_host": "auto",
    "_channel_doc": "OPTIONAL — names and policy of the agent-mail channel between the supervision roles (Supervisor, TeamLead, Advisor, Reviewer), shared by the whole team; machine paths never go here (they live in the gitignored .agents/team/channel.local.json written by /setup:teams → channel). project_code: the registry key and message label for this project. mail_suffix: appended to every role name to form the recipient name (Advisor + XY = AdvisorXY), so two projects on one machine never share a recipient; may be empty. tmux_prefix: prefix of the role sessions' tmux names (default: the repo dir slug). advisor: pair (a Reviewer on a second-vendor model co-signs tier-2 decisions) | solo (the Advisor decides alone, every note says reviewer: none). Notifications are not configured here: ~/.agent-comms/notify.json (.claude/lib/notify.sh). Defaults when a key is absent: mail_suffix empty, tmux_prefix the repo dir slug, advisor pair when codex is true else solo. Read by .claude/lib/team-channel.sh (team_channel_load), never parsed elsewhere. Schema and protocol: .agents/reference/agent-mail.md.",
    "_policy_doc": "OPTIONAL — project policy of the supervision model; every key optional, an absent key = the conservative behaviour named here, never another project's values. The values below are one project's choices, shown as examples to adopt consciously. sensitive_paths: globs whose changes make a team question tier 2 (Advisor pair) and a QA ready-with-warnings the Advisor's disposition; absent = the list in CLAUDE.md → Validation → Test policy. A judgement input for the TeamLead and the Advisor (attested, not proven) — no script enforces it yet. classes: the deterministic tier-1 waivers of an ask-tier prompt (merge_line, allow_offline, add_ask, prod_diagnose — prod_diagnose also needs dir, a relative directory of read-only diagnose scripts, and privileged, a regex of the subcommands that are not); deny, egress, the SQL guard and unreadable prompts are never waived. Each class is OFF until its enabled is exactly true here; the Operator's runtime switch (classes.<name> \"off\" in .agents/team/watch/state.json, by hand) can then narrow it, never widen it. prod_diagnose also needs dir and privileged. pr: self_approve (default false) lets /pr-merge vote approve on the user's own PR and arm auto-complete; off = it stops and names the missing approval or the running policy. tickets: labels and lines on tickets the Supervisor/Advisor create from recommendations; absent = no labels, the recommendation line is still written. closure: automatic ticket-closure rules the Supervisor applies; absent = no automatic closure. Human overview: docs/teams/06-permissions.md (starter repo).",
    "policy": {
      "sensitive_paths": ["src/payments/**", "src/auth/**", "src/webhooks/**", "src/licensing/**"],
      "classes": {
        "merge_line": {"enabled": false},
        "allow_offline": {"enabled": false},
        "add_ask": {"enabled": false}
      },
      "pr": {"self_approve": false},
      "tickets": {
        "auto_label": "auto-created",
        "path_labels": {"brainstorm": "brainstorm", "quick": "quick-change", "team": "team", "analysis": "analysis"},
        "recommendation_line": "Created from a recommendation — the Operator verifies it when the work starts",
        "new_epic_when_none_fits": true
      },
      "closure": {
        "done_after_release_minutes_without_5xx": 40,
        "dry_run_max_days": 7,
        "on_hold_after_days": 7
      }
    },
    "channel": {
      "project_code": "XY",
      "mail_suffix": "XY",
      "tmux_prefix": "xy",
      "advisor": "pair"
    },
    "_teamlead_doc": "OPTIONAL — the per-run TeamLead: one lead session per brainstorm or quick run (never in mode cli) that gates the plan against the order, answers the team's questions, accepts the result against the order brief and escalates only human-only decisions to the Operator. human_only: rule groups whose prompts only the Operator answers — settings-profiles (settings and permission-profile changes) and prod-ask (the project's production ask rules); default both. rework_budget.brainstorm / .quick: rework rounds after a rejected acceptance before the Operator decides (defaults 2 and 1). revise_limit: revise verdicts per gate before the lead must escalate (default 3, at least 1). The values below equal the defaults. Contract: .agents/reference/team-lead.md.",
    "teamlead": {
      "human_only": ["settings-profiles", "prod-ask"],
      "rework_budget": {"brainstorm": 2, "quick": 1},
      "revise_limit": 3
    },
    "_sessions_doc": "OPTIONAL — the project's role sessions, read by .claude/lib/roles-lib.sh (the one reader) for /roles (roles.sh), the SessionStart contract line and the per-run TeamLead. One key per role, name ^[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]{1,23}$; a project adds or replaces a role by editing this block, never the code. Fields: cli claude (default) | codex; model (a model name or alias; a codex role's \"default\" — or no model — starts Codex without -m, on the Codex default model of the role's environment (model in $CODEX_HOME/config.toml); name a model to pin it; claude roles always name one); effort low | medium | high | xhigh | max; settings (Claude only: a .claude/team/sessions/<name>.settings.json template, materialised per role with env TEAM_SESSION_ROLE = the role); contract (a repo-relative file: the hook names it to a Claude role, a Codex role gets it on its launch line); place {tab, slot main | side} (at most one main per tab; Herdr placement); standing (default true; false = per-run configuration nobody starts from /roles — the TeamLead, whose model and effort the runner reads, default fable · medium); enabled (default true). Every rule is checked before /roles starts anything. Absent block = the four roles below. The old shape ({model, effort} only) still reads, with these values as defaults for the missing fields; a legacy Advisor key is read as Fable with a warning. A running window picks up a change only after /roles stop <Role> and /roles.",
    "sessions": {
      "Supervisor": {"cli": "claude", "model": "opus", "effort": "high", "settings": "Supervisor",
                     "contract": ".agents/reference/roles/supervisor.md", "place": {"tab": "Supervisor", "slot": "main"}},
      "Fable": {"cli": "claude", "model": "fable", "effort": "medium", "settings": "Advisor",
                "contract": ".agents/reference/roles/advisor.md", "place": {"tab": "Supervisor", "slot": "side"}},
      "Astra": {"cli": "codex", "model": "default", "effort": "high",
                "contract": ".agents/reference/roles/advisor.md", "place": {"tab": "Supervisor", "slot": "side"}},
      "TeamLead": {"standing": false, "cli": "claude", "model": "fable", "effort": "medium"}
    }
  },

  "_jira_doc": "Jira sync settings. estimates: none | timetracking | <custom field id> — none = no time estimate is sent (sizes stay in the description); absent = the field remembered in .agents/memory/domain/jira.md, else $JIRA_ESTIMATE_FIELD, else timetracking. commit_drafts: reserved — committing .agents/jira/ is unsupported in v1. started_status: the Jira status the start's claim transitions a won ticket to (/start-team, /start-task); absent = asked once on the first start, then saved here.",
  "jira": { "estimates": "timetracking", "commit_drafts": false, "started_status": "In Progress" },

  "_ba_doc": "BA profile. enabled: a Business Analyst runs discovery and the backlog in this repo (written by /setup:start later; nothing reads it yet). meetings_dir: where raw transcripts are dropped for /meeting-notes. vendor / client: participant-group labels in meeting notes (defaults Vendor / Client). Absent block = BA profile off.",
  "ba": { "enabled": false, "meetings_dir": ".agents/sources/meetings/", "vendor": "", "client": "" },

  "_delivery_doc": "Delivery-plan settings, read by /setup:delivery-plan (which writes `mode` and the numbers you answer) and .claude/lib/delivery-plan.mjs (flags override). mode: solo | team. velocity: points per person per sprint — pessimistic and optimal — and sprint_days, the working days of one sprint. solo.sessions / team.*_sessions: parallel sessions of one person (integers 1–6); sessions split that person's throughput, they do not add to it. team: developers (>= 1) join when CORE is done; tl_core_sessions = the Tech Lead's sessions during CORE; tl_scale_sessions and tl_scale_share (0 < share <= 1) = the Tech Lead after the team scales; core_developers: auto (one developer joins CORE when that cuts it by at least 20 %) | 0 | 1. Absent block = the defaults shown here.",
  "delivery": {
    "mode": "team",
    "velocity": { "pessimistic": 64, "optimal": 96, "sprint_days": 10 },
    "solo": { "sessions": 3 },
    "team": { "developers": 2, "developer_sessions": 3, "tl_core_sessions": 3, "tl_scale_sessions": 2,
              "tl_scale_share": 0.5, "core_developers": "auto" }
  },

  "_upkeep_doc": "Upkeep reminders, read by .claude/lib/upkeep.sh (/prime, /tasks-status). Asked by /setup:start (Screen 2, Q8); absent = these defaults; false = off. cleanup_workflow: due when commits since its last recorded run >= commits AND days >= min_days. architecture_review: the same with commits whose subject starts with feat (feat_commits). refresh_brief: true = due when docs/PRD.md's content differs from the one recorded at the last refresh. An item set to false is off. commits and feat_commits are integers >= 1, min_days >= 0. Each command records its run in .agents/upkeep.json (commit it). The template's own checkout keeps .agents/upkeep.json in .git/info/exclude.",
  "upkeep": {
    "cleanup_workflow": { "commits": 200, "min_days": 14 },
    "architecture_review": { "_repo_doc": "Another checkout (relative to this one) whose history and .agents/upkeep.json decide this item, for a repo whose workflow is developed elsewhere; empty = this repo.", "feat_commits": 40, "min_days": 14, "repo": "" },
    "refresh_brief": true
  },

  "_commands_doc": "The command-group manifest, DERIVED from the answers above and shown for confirmation. false => /setup:start offers to prune the group (files + coupled edits) and records the deleted paths in .claude/.starter-sync.json → excluded. qa_runtime_ui is true whenever app_surface is unknown or web. ba = the BA command group (meeting-notes, update-backlog, check-backlog, resync) — pruning arrives with the /setup:start interview. herdr = /maintain:sync-herdr-kit, the opt-in installer of the Herdr panels kit; derived from its existing value, else true (a no-op without a Herdr app home).",
  "commands": {
    "pr": true,
    "jira": false,
    "confluence": false,
    "codex": false,
    "qa_runtime_ui": true,
    "herdr": true,
    "ba": false
  }
}
